## Summary

  - Convert player ground orders into attack-move orders that engage nearby visible enemies and then
    resume their original formation destination.

  - Require shared red-team terrain visibility before enemies can acquire, chase, or shoot player
    units.

  - Optimize the measured hotspots—navigation, visibility, targeting, and terrain rendering—to sustain
    60 FPS with 100 total living units.

  ## Implementation Changes

  - Add a persistent player order_pos and target-source state to Unit, separating the player’s
    destination from the temporary combat chase destination.

  - Acquire visible enemies within the existing 5-tile player radius even while following an order.
    Retain an automatic target while visible within a 6-tile hysteresis radius; after it dies, becomes
    hidden, or escapes, select another nearby target or resume order_pos.

  - Maintain cached visible-cell sets for both teams using identical terrain sight rules. Red combat
    uses shared red-team visibility; strategic AI may remember last-seen information but cannot update
    an unseen unit’s position.

  - When red vision is lost, clear the live target reference, preserve only its last visible position,
    and prevent attack() from applying damage without current team visibility.

  - Replace whole-army targeting scans with spatial-hash radius queries, including nearby-archer and
    threat scoring.

  - Cache terrain LOS results by terrain revision, observer grid cell, and sight budget; reuse them for
    player fog, red vision, and strategic knowledge.

  - Stop rebuilding full dynamic occupancy for every moving unit and waypoint check. Build team
    occupancy once per movement snapshot, validate cached routes with spatial corridor queries, and let
    attack-move engagement/local separation handle moving opposing troops.

  - Queue new A* requests deterministically by unit UID, permit at most two searches per update, and
    use direct steering while queued so dense formations cannot create a one-frame pathfinding spike.

  - Render terrain from a cached composite surface and blit only the viewport instead of issuing
    thousands of tile blits every frame; rebuild it only when the level or zoom changes.

  ## Interfaces and Compatibility

  - Extend Unit with order_pos: Optional[tuple[float, float]] and automatic/explicit target-source
    state.

  - Add an internal team-aware visibility query such as is_team_visible(team, x, y) while retaining
    visible, explored, and is_visible() as the green player-facing fog interface.

  - Preserve explicit attack orders, enemy strategic states, formation slots, terrain movement rules,
    deterministic UID ordering, and saved-free runtime behavior.

  ## Test Plan

  - Replace the current “player order prevents idle auto attack” expectation with tests proving
    acquisition during movement, original-destination preservation, target-switching, leash hysteresis,
    and order resumption after death, concealment, or escape.

  - Add forest tests proving that no red unit can acquire or damage an unseen player, another red
    observer can provide shared vision, and lost targets are pursued only to their last visible
    location.

  - Add final attack-time visibility tests so stale target references cannot bypass fog rules.
  - Add structural performance tests for bounded spatial candidate counts, one occupancy build per
    team/update, cached LOS reuse, deterministic A* queuing, and the two-search update limit.

  - Expand simulate_performance.py with a reproducible dense-battle scenario containing exactly 100
    total units, fixed 60 Hz updates, rendering, fog, combat targeting, and navigation. After warm-up,
    require average frame work at or below 16.67 ms and p95 at or below 20 ms on the reference
    environment.

  - Run all existing tests after implementation; the current baseline is 345 passing tests in 187.5
    seconds.

  ## Assumptions

  - “100 units” includes both armies, kings, and guards combined.
  - Enemy combat visibility is shared across the red team.
  - Player ground orders are attack-move orders: temporary combat does not discard the destination.
  - Enemy strategic AI may use legitimate last-seen memory, but unseen live positions and attacks are
    forbidden.
